---
/* ============================================================================
   Nozzle — site-wide AI search affordance (Phase 3 §2 of ai-features-v1)
   ============================================================================
   Persistent fixture on the lower-left edge of the viewport, visually riding
   the scroll indicator's track. Future interactions (per
   decisions/002-homepage-marquee-and-nozzle-affordance.md §4):
     - Click → slide horizontally to mid-viewport (task #2)
     - Steam emits from tip and grows to fill viewport (task #3 —
       SteamTransition.tsx React island)
     - Search modal materializes through the steam (task #4 —
       SearchModal.tsx React island)
     - After modal closes → nozzle slides back to resting position
     - Affordance scope at v1: triggers AI semantic search modal
     - Affordance scope at v1.1: triggers unified search+chat surface

   This file covers ONLY the resting/visual state — the button + image + scroll
   coupling. Interactions are added in follow-up commits.

   Position rationale: lower-left above the scrollbar gives the nozzle a
   "fixture mounted on the side of the machine" quality per the steampunk
   vocabulary. Vertical motion correlated with scroll position reads as
   "worldful object riding the laser" rather than floating UI chrome.

   Asset: nozzle-detailed.png (bronze + verdigris variant from the 2026-05-06
   visual-direction calibration — matches the redesign's warm-bronze palette).
   97 × 104 px source, displayed at 2.5rem. The ambient steam-puff idle
   (per the same calibration) is a future polish item, not in this v1 pass.

   Accessibility: <button> with aria-label "Open AI search" and aria-haspopup
   "dialog". Keyboard focus visible via focus-visible outline. No global
   keyboard shortcut (Cmd/Ctrl+K explicitly retired per decisions/ai-features-v1
   amended decision 10 — the nozzle is the deliberate visible surface).
*/
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import nozzleImg from '~/assets/images/nozzle-detailed.png';
---
<button
  class="site-nozzle"
  type="button"
  aria-label="Open AI search"
  aria-haspopup="dialog"
  aria-expanded="false"
  data-state="resting"
>
  <Image src={nozzleImg} alt="" class="site-nozzle-img" loading="eager" />
</button>

<style>
  /* Fixed left-edge fixture. The nozzle visually "rides" the scroll indicator
     on the left — its vertical position is set by JS in BaseLayout's existing
     scroll rAF tick, mirroring the scrollbar thumb's progress. Reads as a
     worldful object mounted on the scrollbar laser, not floating UI chrome.

     Stacking: z-index 30 sits BELOW the scroll indicator (40), the footer
     (50), and the header (100). The scroll indicator's cyan laser paints
     IN FRONT of the nozzle's base — reads as "nozzle mounted behind the
     laser fixture" rather than "nozzle floating in front of the
     scrollbar". The footer also paints in front, which is belt-and-
     suspenders to the Y-range cap below (the nozzle's vertical range
     stops well above where the footer sits anyway — see updateNozzleY()
     in BaseLayout). Sean direction 2026-05-22 iter 1. */
  .site-nozzle {
    position: fixed;
    /* Anchor at top:0; vertical position is driven entirely by transform
       (set via CSS var --nozzle-y from the BaseLayout scroll-tick JS).
       Default before JS runs: parks 80vh down so the nozzle is visible
       even with no script. The fallback uses vh intentionally — the
       nozzle's "out-of-flow fixed fixture" role makes viewport-anchored
       sizing the correct choice (one of the documented exceptions to the
       prefer-percentages rule). */
    top: 0;
    /* `left` mirrors the .scroll-indicator EXACTLY (same property value
       per-tier as the scrollbar) — Sean direction 2026-05-22 iter 4. The
       earlier `calc(3% + 3px)` / `calc((100% - --main-max-width)/2 + 3px)`
       values were intended to put the nozzle's left edge at the
       scrollbar's centerline (scrollbar centerline = scrollbar.left + 3px),
       but CSS transition interpolation between calc() expressions of
       different structures (e.g. `3px` → `calc(3% + 3px)`) doesn't
       interpolate identically to the scrollbar's simpler `0` → `3%`
       across the same 300ms — visible "nozzle ahead of scrollbar"
       desync at breakpoint crossings.
       Fix: the nozzle's `left` now matches the scrollbar's `left` exactly
       at every tier, and the +3px offset to the scrollbar's centerline
       is applied via `margin-left: 3px` (a constant offset, never
       transitions). Both elements interpolate the SAME `left` formula at
       the SAME 300ms ease-out, guaranteeing identical motion. */
    left: 0;
    /* Constant 5px shift positions the nozzle's box-left INSIDE the
       scrollbar's right edge (scrollbar is 6px wide at left:0), so the
       leftmost 1px of the nozzle's box is behind the scrollbar — the
       scrollbar (z 40) occludes any image artifact at the nozzle's left
       edge. The visible rotated image extends from box-left to box-right
       (full button width); putting box-left at x=5 means the image's
       leftmost ~1px is hidden behind the scrollbar, and any drop-shadow
       halo extending into x < 0 is clipped by body's overflow-x. Iter
       history:
       - 3px (SPR-0034/35): box-left at scrollbar centerline; image's
         leftmost pixels visible to the LEFT of the scrollbar — wrong
       - 7px (SPR-0041): pulled nozzle ~6px inward but now there was a
         visible gap + artifact between nozzle's left edge and
         scrollbar's right edge (1px gap at x=6 → x=7)
       - 5px (this commit, SPR-0042): box-left INSIDE scrollbar range,
         leftmost 1px occluded by scrollbar — artifact hidden, recess
         visual preserved. Sean direction 2026-05-22 iter 6: "the
         starting nozzle position is one or two pixels more to the left" */
    margin-left: 5px;
    /* transform composes the slide-to-mid X (from --nozzle-x, default 0)
       and the scroll-coupled Y (from --nozzle-y, set by BaseLayout's
       scroll tick). Resting state keeps --nozzle-x at 0 so the nozzle
       sits at its tier-anchored `left` position. The active state below
       overrides --nozzle-x with a per-tier translate value that slides
       the nozzle's center to viewport-x=50%. translateY tracks scroll
       in both states (with active pausing the JS update via the
       data-state check in BaseLayout's updateNozzleY). */
    transform: translate(var(--nozzle-x, 0), var(--nozzle-y, 80vh));
    width: 2.5rem;
    /* Explicit height so the rotation in .site-nozzle-img rotates within
       a fixed-size box rather than altering the bounding-box geometry.
       Square 2.5rem × 2.5rem holds the nozzle PNG (97×104, near-square)
       regardless of orientation. */
    height: 2.5rem;
    padding: 0;
    border: 0;
    background: transparent;
    cursor: pointer;
    z-index: 30;
    /* Slide transitions:
       - `transform` (170ms ease-out): the click → recess animation. Was
         250ms in iter 6-10; cut to 170ms in iter 12 (Sean: "cut the total
         runtime of the animation opening by a third") so the recess scales
         in lockstep with the steam animation that follows. The 67% scale
         factor (= 2/3) applies to all opening-animation durations across
         Nozzle.astro and SteamTransition.css.
         Scroll-driven Y updates would lag at 170ms ease-out so the JS
         toggles data-scrolling="true" during active scroll to disable the
         transition temporarily — re-enabled 150ms after scroll settles.
       - `left` (300ms ease-out): matches the responsive-ladder transition
         timing on .scroll-indicator + main (both use `300ms ease-out` for
         their left/max-width changes at the 896 and 1520 breakpoints).
         NOT scaled — this is breakpoint-crossing chrome, not part of the
         click-to-open animation. */
    transition:
      transform 170ms ease-out,
      left 300ms ease-out;
  }

  /* Disable the transform transition during scroll so the rAF-driven Y
     updates don't lag behind a 250ms ease-out interpolation. The data-
     scrolling attribute is toggled by BaseLayout's scroll tick (set on
     scroll AND resize events; cleared 150ms after last event).
     CRITICAL: keep the `left` transition active so breakpoint-crossing
     resizes still slide the nozzle in lockstep with the scrollbar + main
     (both transition `left`/`max-width` at 300ms ease-out). The earlier
     `transition: none` blanket-disabled left too, causing the nozzle's
     horizontal position to snap on resize while the scrollbar slid —
     Sean direction 2026-05-22 iter 3. Now: transform has no transition
     during scroll (rAF handles smoothness); left keeps its 300ms ease-out. */
  .site-nozzle[data-scrolling="true"] {
    transition: left 300ms ease-out;
  }

  .site-nozzle-img {
    display: block;
    /* Width + height 100% with object-fit:contain fits the PNG (97×104)
       inside the 2.5rem square button preserving aspect ratio. After the
       90deg rotation below, the visual content still fits in the same
       square box (the rotation pivots around the box's center). */
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: contain;
    /* Rotate 90° clockwise so the nozzle's opening (top of the source
       PNG) faces RIGHT toward the viewport center, not UP toward the
       header. Reads as a fixture firing inward from the scrollbar pipe.
       Sean direction 2026-05-22 iter 1. */
    transform: rotate(90deg);
    /* Brass/copper drop-shadow with a slight upward bias (1px y-offset
       upward) so the glow reads as "steam rising from the nozzle's tip"
       even at rest — visual continuity with the steam animation that
       fires on click. Sean direction 2026-05-22 iter 8. */
    filter: drop-shadow(0 -1px 3px rgba(196, 149, 57, .5)) drop-shadow(0 0 8px rgba(196, 149, 57, .25));
    /* Subtle interactive feedback on hover/focus — brightens the glow.
       Click → modal flow is task #2 (slide animation) + tasks #3-6
       (steam + modal). For now hover is a hint that the nozzle is
       interactive. */
    transition: filter 200ms ease-out;
  }

  .site-nozzle:hover .site-nozzle-img,
  .site-nozzle:focus-visible .site-nozzle-img {
    /* Single upward-biased halo on hover/focus — brighter brass-light tone
       so the "active rising" feel intensifies. Sean direction 2026-05-22
       iter 8. */
    filter: drop-shadow(0px -1px 5px rgba(232, 185, 94, .85));
  }

  /* Keyboard focus ring — circular to match the nozzle's organic shape,
     cyan to match the brand laser color (cyan is reserved for brand-mark
     moments per the palette-split rule; the nozzle is a brand-mark
     fixture). */
  .site-nozzle:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--color-electric-cyan);
    outline-offset: 4px;
    border-radius: 50%;
  }

  /* Tier-responsive left position — matches the .scroll-indicator's
     responsive ladder in global.css EXACTLY at every tier. The +3px
     offset to the scrollbar's centerline is on `margin-left` (above),
     not folded into `left`, so the `left` property animates identically
     to the scrollbar at the 896 and 1520 breakpoint crossings. */
  @media (min-width: 896px) {
    .site-nozzle {
      left: 3%;
    }
  }
  @media (min-width: 1520px) {
    .site-nozzle {
      left: calc((100% - var(--main-max-width)) / 2);
    }
  }

  /* Active state — nozzle recesses slightly LEFT into the scrollbar as
     click feedback. This is a DEVIATION from the original spec
     (decisions/002 §4) which called for "the nozzle slides horizontally
     to the halfway point of the viewport (mid-screen)". The deviation
     arose from a CSS bug discovered in SPR-0043 that Sean ended up
     preferring:

     SPR-0039 through SPR-0042 used `calc(50% - 5px - 1.25rem)` etc. in
     this translate-X, intending "translate by 50% of viewport minus
     constants." BUT `%` in `transform: translate()` resolves against
     the ELEMENT'S OWN SIZE, not the containing block (CSS Transforms
     spec). So 50% of the 2.5rem-wide nozzle = 20px, and the calc
     resolved to 20px - 5px - 20px = -5px — a tiny leftward translation
     instead of a hundreds-of-pixels rightward slide to viewport center.

     Sean's iter-4-through-6 reactions ("recesses into the scrollbar...
     I like this effect") were all describing the buggy behavior. When
     I tried to interpret them as steam-related, they didn't fit; Sean's
     latest clarification ("when you click the nozzle, it moves a few
     pixels to the left, but that shows a couple of pixels of the left
     edge of the nozzle on the left side of the scrollbar") confirms
     the leftward recess IS what's happening AND is what Sean wants.

     Iter-7 tuning per Sean's "stop 2 or 3 pixels sooner than the
     position it currently hits": current recess was -5px (on <896 and
     ≥896 tiers, where %-as-element-width resolved that way) or +725px
     rightward (≥1520, where the calc happened to resolve as pixels and
     genuinely went to viewport center — a different bug, in the other
     direction). Both replaced with a uniform -3px to give the same
     short-recess feedback on every viewport.

     No tier-specific media queries needed since the recess depth is now
     a constant. If the original slide-to-mid-viewport spec is still
     desired, the fix is to use `vw` instead of `%`:
       <896:  calc(50vw - 5px - 1.25rem)
       ≥896:  calc(47vw - 5px - 1.25rem)
       ≥1520: calc(var(--main-max-width) / 2 - 5px - 1.25rem) (this one
              didn't need vw because the gutter halves cancel out to a
              fixed-pixel constant). Documented here so the slide-to-
              center can be reinstated if Sean wants it later — the
              steam + future modal would need their geometry adjusted
              to match (currently the steam gradient is centered at
              calc(50% + 1rem) which assumes the nozzle is at viewport
              center; if the nozzle stays at the scrollbar, the steam's
              center should be at the nozzle's actual position). */
  .site-nozzle[data-state="active"] {
    --nozzle-x: -3px;
  }

  /* Hide on print — nozzle is interactive chrome, not document content. */
  @media print {
    .site-nozzle {
      display: none;
    }
  }

  /* prefers-reduced-motion: keep the click-slide transition (functional
     state change, not decorative motion) but document the intent. The
     steam animation (task #3) WILL respect reduced-motion per
     decisions/002 §4 (simplify to quick fade). */
</style>

<script>
  /* Nozzle interaction — click toggles state between 'resting' and 'active'.
     Active state slides the nozzle to mid-viewport via the CSS rules above.
     The future steam animation + modal mount (tasks #3-6) will key off the
     same 'active' state.

     This script intentionally has zero modal-mounting logic. It only manages
     state. Subsequent tasks add:
       - SteamTransition.tsx React island (task #3) — listens for state
         change and runs the steam animation.
       - SearchModal.tsx React island (task #4) — mounts through steam.

     Decoupling lets us ship + verify each piece in isolation: this commit
     lands click-to-slide-to-mid-and-back with no modal, so Sean can verify
     the slide geometry on staging before more behavior is layered on. */
  (function () {
    const nozzle = document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('.site-nozzle');
    if (!nozzle) return;

    function setState(state: 'resting' | 'active') {
      if (!nozzle) return;
      nozzle.dataset.state = state;
      nozzle.setAttribute('aria-expanded', state === 'active' ? 'true' : 'false');
      if (state === 'resting') {
        /* When sliding back to resting, the BaseLayout scroll tick resumes
           updating --nozzle-y (it had paused via the `data-state !==
           'resting'` check). The nozzle's Y may be stale (scroll position
           changed during active state); dispatch a synthetic scroll event
           so BaseLayout's listener recomputes --nozzle-y for the current
           scroll position. The transition (250ms ease-out on transform)
           animates the Y update in lockstep with the X slide-back. */
        window.dispatchEvent(new Event('scroll'));
      }
    }

    /* Click toggles between resting and active. The aria-haspopup="dialog"
       attribute is left on the button at all times — it announces "this
       opens a dialog" to assistive tech, which is true regardless of
       current state. */
    nozzle.addEventListener('click', () => {
      const next = nozzle.dataset.state === 'active' ? 'resting' : 'active';
      setState(next);
    });

    /* Escape closes (returns to resting) when active. Outside-click handling
       is intentionally NOT here — the search modal (task #4) is what owns
       the click-outside-to-close semantics. For now, with no modal, the
       nozzle's slide-out lives at the viewport center and the user dismisses
       via Escape or clicking the nozzle itself. */
    document.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
      if (e.key === 'Escape' && nozzle.dataset.state === 'active') {
        setState('resting');
      }
    });
  })();
</script>
